    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's weird because the airflow seems okay when I stand right by the vent, but it just doesn't feel cold. i'm worried there's some kind of blockage or something weird going on inside the ducts that i can't see. can you give me a checklist of things i should look for when i go back over there to investigate? maybe some specific signs that it's a mechanical failure vs just a bad vent?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When investigating the issue, here's a checklist of things to look for:\n\n- Check for visible blockages, such as dusty or dirty air filters, pet hair, or debris in the ducts.\n- Look for signs of forced air leaks, such as damaged or loose duct connections, gaps in the ducts, or excessive wear on seams.\n- Inspect the return vent (one end of the duct that draws outside air into the AC) for leaves, branches, or other obstructions that might be restricting airflow.\n- Check the air handler and vent registers for proper sizing and clearance.\n- Verify that the blower motor and fan are working correctly and not clogged with debris.\n\nFor mechanical failure signs, look for:\n\n- Frayed or damaged wiring, loose connections, or exposed electrical components.\n- Leaks in the refrigerant line or other system components.\n- Age and wear on components, such as bendable hoses or corroded connections.\n- Unusual noises, such as vibration or rattling, coming from the AC unit or ductwork.",
